WEBVTT

0
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:06.849
We have spent this course tearing down traditional cloud assumptions.

1
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.520
We have looked at why standard playbooks fail,

2
00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:11.119
how state is distributed,

3
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.189
and the immense physical toll of the coordination tax.

4
00:00:15.149 --> 00:00:16.450
Now it's time to build.

5
00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.750
To keep distributed air reliable at massive scale,

6
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.870
you must establish non-negotiable infrastructure guardrails.

7
00:00:24.879 --> 00:00:26.000
Guard rule number one,

8
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.780
the network is the backplane.

9
00:00:28.809 --> 00:00:32.250
You can no longer treat networking as a flexible data pipe.

10
00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:35.330
It is the literal spine of your compute cluster.

11
00:00:36.139 --> 00:00:40.250
If your network design does not prioritise system coherence alongside bandwidth,

12
00:00:40.659 --> 00:00:43.659
you will encounter scaling and reliability limits.

13
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:45.639
Guardrail number 2,

14
00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.290
consistency over theoretical speed.

15
00:00:48.869 --> 00:00:53.430
We have seen how jitter and latency propagate through a synchronous system.

16
00:00:54.369 --> 00:00:56.750
You must design for absolute predictability.

17
00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.130
A predictable network often delivers better system outcomes than a faster,

18
00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:03.720
but highly variable one.

19
00:01:04.738 --> 00:01:05.948
Guardrail number 3,

20
00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:08.439
strictly manage failure boundaries.

21
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.019
You know that localised failures cascade rapidly.

22
00:01:12.349 --> 00:01:16.180
Your orchestration and automation layers must be designed to halt,

23
00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.190
isolate and recover stateful workloads

24
00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:21.629
without causing a cluster-wide disruption.

25
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.949
Distributed AI forces us to return to the fundamentals of systems engineering.

26
00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:30.040
It is a demanding systems engineering work,

27
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:35.449
but it is the only way to unlock the true potential of these massively scaled models.

28
00:01:36.889 --> 00:01:38.349
Take these principles,

29
00:01:38.769 --> 00:01:40.559
challenge your existing architectures,

30
00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:44.370
and build systems that are actually designed for the realities of AI.

31
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:45.790
Thank you for watching.